---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StackDiagram from "../components/StackDiagram.astro";
import TakeATinCta from "../components/TakeATinCta.astro";
import { tins, statusOf } from "../data/tins";

const unpublished = tins.filter((t) => statusOf(t) !== "shelf").map((t) => t.name);

const description =
  "Every magmalake tin, layer by layer — execution, tables and catalogs, file formats, storage, primitives and utilities — each linking to its own page: what it is, how to install it, what proves it and how fast it is.";
---

<Base title="Tins" description={description}>
  <section class="section section--alt" id="stack">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">The tins</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Every tin, layer by layer</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        At the top, execution: Orca schedules pipelines, Dista runs Mojo functions across a
        cluster, and restate makes a flow durable. Beneath it, Iceberg
        tables and their catalogs; then the file formats a table is made of, and the
        storage and databases it lives in; then the codecs, hashes and threads they all
        share, and small utilities beside them. I kept every piece separate, so you can
        take just the one you need.
      </p>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        magmalake's own tins are Apache-2.0 and CI-tested on stable Mojo 1.2.0 and the
        current nightly, across macOS and Linux; the three republished ones keep upstream's
        MIT licence. Every tin but {unpublished.join(" and ")} is published on mojoshelf.
        Each of magmalake's own carries a correctness oracle — an outside implementation I
        made it agree with. Its page says which, with its numbers and its install line.
      </p>
      <StackDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ what this is ============================ -->
  <section class="section" id="what">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this is</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">From a table format to a platform</h2>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            magmalake began when Mojo reached 1.0 as an exploration in building
            a meaningfully large data library in it? — and I picked
            <a href="https://iceberg.apache.org" rel="noopener">Apache Iceberg</a> as the
            target. What came out is a stack rather than a demo.
            Parquet, Avro, Thrift, Iceberg itself, Roaring bitmaps, Snappy and the hashes are
            implemented in Mojo. A few very mature C libraries are linked rather
            than reimplemented — <strong>libzstd</strong>, <strong>liblz4</strong> and
            <strong>libbrotli</strong> for those three codecs, <strong>libcurl</strong> for
            HTTP and TLS, and <strong>pthreads</strong> for the threads the language does not
            yet provide. What is gone is the Python and JVM layer that every other route from
            Mojo to Iceberg has to go through.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then it grew upward. <a href="/s/orca">Orca</a> is the orchestration layer:
            pipelines as DAGs in Mojo, checked by the compiler, partitioned by time or arbitrary keys
            and run on cron schedules, with a scheduler that backfills and retries. It loads
            every month of NYC taxi trips since 2011, 1.44 billion rows, four runs at a time.
            <a href="/s/dista">Dista</a>, beneath it, runs Mojo functions distributed across a cluster.
            On those taxi rows in S3 it
            beats both Ray and a Rust implementation of the same queries.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="callout-stack">
        <aside class="callout">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Packaged on mojoshelf</h3>
          <p>
            Magmalake leans on the packaging infrastructure defined by
            <a href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">mojoshelf.org</a> — a registry for
            Mojo <em>tins</em> on top of pixi. Nearly every tin here is published there and
            installable with one command.
          </p>
          <a class="callout__link" href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">
            mojoshelf.org
            <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"
                 stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M3 9 9 3M4.2 3H9v4.8" />
            </svg>
          </a>
        </aside>

        <aside class="callout" id="skills">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Skills for coding agents</h3>
          <p>
            What magmalake learned making its code fast is packaged as an agent skill, so an
            agent writing a data pipeline in Mojo starts with it. Each rule carries the
            measurement behind it.
          </p>
          <pre class="callout__code"><code>npx skills add magmalake/skills</code></pre>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            That installs it. In Claude Code you can instead run{" "}
            <code>/plugin marketplace add magmalake/skills</code> and then{" "}
            <code>/plugin install skills@magmalake</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/skills/tree/main/mojo-data-performance" rel="noopener"><code>mojo-data-performance</code></a>
            covers a pipeline's per-row loops and the readers, writers and codecs under
            them, how to tell the time spent decoding from the time spent on your own work
            without a second implementation to compare against, and how to take a
            benchmark number that survives being checked.
          </p>
        </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt" id="take">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <TakeATinCta />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
